      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "$$\n(x+1){y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?\n\n$\\,$\n${W}^{′}(x)=$ {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by $x+1$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{2x}{x+1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{x+1}y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2x}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2x}{x+1}W(x).\n$$",
